<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seru Epenisa Cakobau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1815–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ratu Seru Cakobau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seru Epenisa Cakobau was the paramount chief of Bau, a small island state off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viti-levu">Viti Levu</a> that dominated much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> in the mid-19th century. He converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1854, and in 1871 he became king of a united Kingdom of Fiji. Burdened by debts and unrest, he ceded Fiji to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1874.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seru-epenisa-cakobau.jpg" alt="Seru Epenisa Cakobau" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francis Herbert Dufty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cakobau,_died_February_1883,_photograph_by_Francis_H._Dufty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
